= Research quality
Different approaches of science provide different forms of knowlege or "evidence-levels"

Strong theoretical linkages are precisely defined and wellgrounded with good variables that measures these consepts

== Quantitative criteria
We start of with validity, that is, does the variables actually measure the construct of interest (do they acutally measure what we care about)

If we have no validity, the test/prediction not therorically sound 

For good quality we aim towards the following

- Theoretical knowlege!
- The research should capture the content that you are interested in
- The study should create a situation where i can test my predictions and draw conclusions based on those prediction
- Reliability: The same measures should get the same results
  - Measurements should be reliable (split-half, like train/test)
- Objectivity: Someone else doing the same test with new data, should come to the same conclusions (there is a "true" value/effect)
  - Researchers should not be biased
  - Participants often wants want so answer "correct"
  - Science can easily be (fx politically) biased when using participants
- We then use external validity
  - This is usually discussed in "limitations" sections

== Qualitative criteria
In qualitative we accept much more bias from the researchers themselves, however credibility plays a bigger role

- Aligment between theory, research questions, data collection and analytical steps
- How is data collected, is it trustworthy and are there enough data
- Saturation (is the research just enough, or did it reach further)
- Transparency is key. You want to have agreement between humans
- Conformability: there should be a clear link between data and interpretation
  - This way other people can easily confirm your analysis
  - So we use clear descriptions of the research was perfomed, as well as reflextion.

